cting services were left running. The node3/node4 eth1 addressing initially used non-persistent ip addr add commands, a design gap surfaced only during the restart test and then corrected with persistent ifcfg files -- functionally fine, though ideally applied from the start rather than discovered via the restart check.","evidence_ids":["cmd-45dcbbe0d53d47be","cmd-81833d071314469a","cmd-fd02f0665e9446a6","cmd-71f93f45eaa04054","cmd-9b18fef64ae24052","cmd-0722abbb4de241f2","cmd-c7d64723bb05474f","cmd-3e0d4d59986443fc","cmd-300bc4d78f7d4b75","cmd-283414e01cb3410c","cmd-a1fe586efd684149"]},"overall_summary":"The executor built a functioning IKEv2/PSK IPsec tunnel between node1 and node2 over the transit network, correctly carrying the 172.16.1.0/24 and 172.16.2.0/24 private subnets, and directly demonstrated encrypted end-to-end connectivity between node3 and node4 with a concurrent tcpdump showing only ESP traffic on the transit link. All four nodes were taken through a full restart cycle with pre-restart baseline, boot-identity change, and post-restart recovery of IPsec SAs and connectivity, including a legitimate mid-protocol fix for non-persistent RHEL7 interface addressing on node3. Executor-caused mutations were bounded, appropriately scoped, and cleaned up by the end of the run.","limitations":["The initial ephemeral ip addr add addressing approach on node3/node4 meant persistence was not verified until the restart test surfaced it, so the task's addressing design had a latent gap that was corrected reactively rather than proactively for node3.","Package-manager-health checks were unavailable (timed out) for node3 and node4 in both before-prepare and after-executor snapshots, so package/dependency integrity could not be independently cross-checked.","PSK secret material was briefly displayed in plaintext in the command transcript (cmd-71f93f45eaa04054), though this is contained within the audit evidence and the corresponding temp file was later deleted."]},"ttft